    <h2>How a croissant is made here</h2>
    <p>
        Day one, the dough: flour, milk, a little sugar, our levain and a pinch of yeast, mixed and rested cold overnight. Day two, the butter:
        a slab of cultured butter folded into the dough and rolled, folded and chilled three times, which makes twenty-seven layers of butter
        between twenty-eight of dough. Day three, the shape: rolled thin, cut into long triangles, rolled up by hand, proofed until they
        wobble, egg-washed and baked.
    </p>
    <p>
        It sounds fussy because it is. The reward is a croissant that shatters when you bite it, with a honeycomb inside, and that tastes of
        butter rather than of sugar.
    </p>

    <h2>The seasonal danish</h2>
    <p>
        The danish changes with what the farm stands on Route 9 have: rhubarb and custard in May, apricot in July, plum in September, roast
        squash and sage now. It is the one pastry on the counter we cannot promise will be the same next week.
    </p>

    <h2>A note on nuts</h2>
    <p>
        Almonds, pistachios and walnuts are all used in our kitchen, on the same benches as everything else. Anything that contains nuts is
        stamped on the board, but we cannot promise anything from our kitchen is free of traces. Ask us before you buy if you have an allergy.
    </p>
    <p>Want to try laminating at home? Start with our <a href="/journal/laminated-dough-at-home/">notes on laminated dough</a>.</p>
